# Target C-II — the close-normal common-run branch forces geometry or cycle rank

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; optimized continuation of the close-normal shared-skeleton route  
**Scope:** small-external `COMMON-LOWER-RUNS` extracted from the close-normal heavy-reuse pair  
**Dependencies:** close-normal shared FREE skeleton; common-run parity refinement; two-normal common-trunk cone theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_close_pair_common_run_geometry_cycle_fork_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The close-pair extraction supplies both a lower and an upper angular scale. Combining those bounds with the exact two-path cycle-rank identity allows the common-trunk threshold to be optimized for Euclidean displacement rather than for selected-edge count.

Assume

\[
Z_D=o(\sqrt D)
\]

and define

\[
R_D:={D^{1/4}\over\sqrt{Z_D+1}}\to\infty.
\tag{GC.1}
\]

Use the tuned slow scale

\[
\kappa_D=D^{1/8}(Z_D+1)^{1/4}.
\tag{GC.2}
\]

Then

\[
m=\Theta(R_D^{1/2}),
\qquad
\gamma_D=\Omega(R_D^{-1/2}),
\tag{GC.3}
\]

where `gamma_D` is the consecutive normal gap.

Run heavy reuse with threshold

\[
R=\lfloor\sqrt m\rfloor.
\]

The close-pair theorem gives two modules with

\[
\boxed{
\Omega(R_D^{-1/2})
\le\theta:=\angle(N_i,N_j)
\le O(R_D^{-1/4}).
}
\tag{GC.4}
\]

After the existing degree-six matching, activation-time thinning, orientation thinning, monotone-subsequence extraction, and lower-run-pair pigeonhole, the two fixed all-lower paths share an ordered vertex-disjoint matching of

\[
\boxed{
q=\Omega(R_D^{3/2})
}
\tag{GC.5}

inverse-`D` lower gates.

The order/orientation parity theorem now gives the following sharpened alternatives.

## 1. PARITY-MISMATCH

No intersection component can contain two selected edges, so

\[
\boxed{
\beta(P\cup Q)\ge q-1=\Omega(R_D^{3/2}).
}
\tag{GC.6}

This is the strongest cycle-rank branch.

## 2. SAME + SAME-ORDER

Let

\[
a:=2\sin(\theta/2).
\]

For every threshold `s>=1`, the common-run theorem and cone theorem imply either

\[
\beta(P\cup Q)\ge q/s-1,
\tag{GC.7}
\]

or one common trunk contains at least `s` selected matching edges and hence has endpoint bisector displacement

\[
X\cdot B>(2s-1)\sin(\theta/2)
=as-\sin(\theta/2).
\tag{GC.8}
\]

Choose

\[
s=\sqrt{q/a}.
\tag{GC.9}
\]

Integer rounding changes only lower-order terms. Both branches are then on the common scale

\[
\sqrt{aq}.
\]

By (GC.4),

\[
a=\Omega(R_D^{-1/2}),
\]

and by (GC.5),

\[
\sqrt{aq}=\Omega(R_D^{1/2}).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{either }\beta(P\cup Q)=\Omega(R_D^{1/2})
\text{ or one SAME/SAME trunk has }X\cdot B=\Omega(R_D^{1/2}).
}
\tag{GC.10}

This improves the naive `s=sqrt(q)` same-direction geometric guarantee, because the threshold is tuned to the small bisector progress per trunk edge.

## 3. OPPOSITE + REVERSE-ORDER

Here the close-normal upper bound is favorable. Since

\[
\theta=O(R_D^{-1/4})=o(1),
\]

one has

\[
\cos(\theta/2)=1-o(1).
\]

Take the balanced threshold

\[
s=\sqrt q.
\]

Then either

\[
\boxed{
\beta(P\cup Q)=\Omega(R_D^{3/4})
}
\tag{GC.11}

or one opposite/reverse common trunk has

\[
\boxed{
X\cdot T=\Omega(R_D^{3/4}).
}
\tag{GC.12}

The close-normal slack theorem adds substantially more structure in the trunk case:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\subset C}(|e|-1)=o(1),
}
\tag{GC.13}

and if `S_C` is the polygonal length of the trunk,

\[
\boxed{
1\le{S_C\over|X_C|}
\le\sec(\theta/2)
=1+O(R_D^{-1/2}).
}
\tag{GC.14}

Thus the opposite/reverse trunk is a diverging, asymptotically straight, nearly unit-spaced chain of old fully inactive outer-face source points.

---

## Abstract minimax used in the SAME/SAME branch

Suppose a common-run fork has `q` selected matching edges and every `s`-edge common trunk would force geometric displacement at least

\[
as-O(a)
\]

for some `a>0`. The complementary cycle-rank branch is at least

\[
q/s-1.
\]

Balancing the two terms by

\[
s=\sqrt{q/a}
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
\max\{\text{forced trunk displacement},\text{forced cycle rank}\}
\ge\sqrt{aq}-O(1+a).
}
\tag{GC.15}
\]

For SAME/SAME, `a=2sin(theta/2)`. For OPPOSITE/REVERSE one may take `a=2cos(theta/2)`, which is asymptotically two for the close pair and recovers the `sqrt(q)` scale.

The result does not close C-II. It replaces the close-pair common-run survivor by explicit diverging geometric or topological quantities on every parity class. The weakest compatible case is now SAME/SAME, where either cycle rank or actual bisector displacement diverges at least as `R_D^(1/2)`.

C-II remains open. No theorem-state authority is changed.

**Disposition:** optimized the common-trunk threshold against two-normal cone progress and obtained a complete close-pair parity fork: `R_D^(3/2)` cycle rank under parity mismatch, `R_D^(1/2)` cycle-rank-or-bisector drift under SAME/SAME, and `R_D^(3/4)` cycle-rank-or-nearly-geodesic transverse drift under OPPOSITE/REVERSE.
